          "text": "Shame on you for being fake news.\n\n‼️👇🏻‼️\n\nNo, the map and claims in the post are not accurate. They depict a fictional scenario of major Ukrainian advances and a Russian collapse in the Pokrovsk sector of Donetsk Oblast, with Ukrainian reconnaissance reportedly spotted up to 20 km behind Russian lines and Russian brigades (such as the 36th and 5th) under severe pressure. In reality, the situation is largely reversed: Russian forces have been conducting slow but sustained offensive operations in this area for months, gradually advancing into northern and northwestern Pokrovsk and nearby settlements like Myrnohrad (also known as Myrnograd), while Ukrainian forces are defending remaining positions under heavy pressure.\nRecent assessments from multiple sources confirm ongoing Russian assaults with limited or no confirmed advances on specific days, but overall territorial gains favoring Russia since early 2026. For instance:\n•  As of February 9, 2026, Russian forces continued attacks around Pokrovsk but made no confirmed advances, with Ukrainian counterattacks noted near Sukhetske and Rodynske.  Ukrainian defenses hold the northern part of the city, but Russia claims full control, which Kyiv denies.  \n•  Russia is nearing capture of Pokrovsk and Myrnohrad, with advances averaging about 70 meters per day since late 2024, controlling most of Pokrovsk by early 2026.   Russian infantry assaults intensified around Hryshyne (northwest of Pokrovsk) on February 10. \n•  Earlier in February, Russian forces made small advances in northwestern Pokrovsk and conducted infiltration missions north of the city.   By February 4, Russia had captured Myrnohrad. \n•  The broader trend shows Russia pressing into urban areas with artillery and drones, while Ukraine struggles to hold lines amid resource constraints.   \nThere are no credible reports of Ukrainian reconnaissance deep behind Russian lines, a breakdown of Russian defenses, or momentum shifting to Ukraine in this sector. The post appears to be based on unverified or fabricated information, and replies to it accuse the author of plagiarizing content from another user (@secretsqrl123), though this doesn’t directly impact the accuracy assessment. War reporting can vary by source bias, but neutral analysts like ISW and Critical Threats consistently describe Russian pressure without evidence of the depicted collapse."
        },
